  "success": true,
  "output": "## Performance Analysis — Canon\n\n**Scope note first:** `engine/` (the actual reconciliation pipeline — blocking, matching, detection, the LangGraph resolution agent, connectors, `db.py`) is 100% unimplemented stubs right now — every function raises `NotImplementedError` (Phase 1/2 per AGENTS.md). There's no real N+1, leak, or redundant computation to find there yet; I've flagged forward-looking risks instead so they're caught during implementation. `web/` is fully built and is where the findings below apply.\n\nOverall the `web/lib/server/*.ts` data layer is already unusually disciplined — batched `Promise.all`, grouped child-count lookups instead of per-row queries, single 5-table joins with aliasing. I did not manufacture problems there; what follows are the real gaps.\n\n---\n\n### 1. N+1 query patterns — none found\n\n`getQueuePage` (`lib/server/conflicts.ts:172`) is the pattern to point to elsewhere: rows, counts, and total run in `Promise.all`, and the per-row child count is **one grouped query** (`countChildrenByParent`) keyed by `sourceId:externalId`, not a query per row. `buildRunExport`, `getAuditPage`, and `getRunDetail` follow the same discipline. Keep this as the house style once `engine/db.py`'s writers are implemented — the equivalent risk there is writing `entities`/`matches`/`conflicts` row-by-row instead of bulk `COPY`/batched `INSERT`.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Unnecessary React re-renders\n\n**Finding: every keystroke in the conflict queue re-renders up to 100 diff rows.**\n\n`ConflictQueue` (`components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx:34`) holds `selectedIndex`/`decided`/`overrideConflictId` in state at the top of the tree. `ConflictDiffRow`, the dense-table row, and `ListRow` are **not** wrapped in `React.memo`, so `j`/`k` navigation re-renders the entire row list (`ConflictSides`, `RationaleBlock`, `ReviewActions`, `SeverityMark` per row) even though only the previously-selected and newly-selected row actually changed visually. This directly undercuts the stated design goal (\"a reviewer working a queue of 400 should never touch the mouse\").\n\nIt's compounded by this, also in `conflict-queue.tsx:58`:\n\n```ts\nconst submitFor = useCallback(\n  (conflictId: string): ReviewSubmit =>\n    (action: ReviewAction) => submit(conflictId, action),\n  [submit],\n);\n// ...\nsubmit={submitFor(row.conflictId)}   // new closure identity every render\n```\n\n`submitFor` is memoized, but calling it per-row inside `.map()` produces a **new function every render**. Even if you add `React.memo` to the row components, this prop breaks the memo immediately because `submit` never has stable identity across renders.\n\n**Fix — make the row itself resolve the callback so the parent hands down stable identifiers, not closures:**\n\n```ts\n// conflict-diff-row.tsx\nexport const ConflictDiffRow = React.memo(function ConflictDiffRow({\n  row, conflictId, onSubmit, /* ...primitives only... */\n}: Props) {\n  const handle = useCallback(\n    (action: ReviewAction) => onSubmit(conflictId, action),\n    [onSubmit, conflictId],\n  );\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-queue.tsx — submit is now the raw, stable function from useReview()\n<ConflictDiffRow\n  key={row.conflictId}\n  row={row}\n  conflictId={row.conflictId}\n  onSubmit={submit}                       // stable reference, not submitFor(id)\n  selected={index === clamped}\n  decidedStatus={decided[row.conflictId]?.status ?? null}\n/>\n```\n\nDo the same for `ConflictDenseTable`'s row (worth extracting into its own component so it can be memoed independently of the header) and `ListRow` in `conflict-focus.tsx`. With `React.memo` + stable callbacks, a `j`/`k` press re-renders 2 rows instead of up to 100.\n\n**Finding: O(n²) render in the ruleset editor.**\n\n`components/rules/ruleset-editor.tsx:166`:\n\n```tsx\n{ordered.map((rule) => {\n  const index = rules.indexOf(rule);        // O(n) — every row, every render\n  return (\n    <RuleRow\n      key={rule.id}\n      position={ordered.indexOf(rule) + 1}   // O(n) again\n      onChange={(patch) => update(index, patch)}\n      ...\n```\n\nEvery keystroke in *any* field calls `setRules`, which re-triggers `orderRulesByEvaluation` and this map — two `indexOf` reference scans per row, so the render is O(n²) in rule count. Rulesets are small today, but it's needless and trivial to fix by carrying the original index through the sort instead of re-deriving it:\n\n```ts\n// types/rules.ts\nexport function orderRulesByEvaluation(rules: SurvivorshipRule[]) {\n  return rules\n    .map((rule, index) => ({ rule, index }))\n    .sort((a, b) => ruleSpecificity(b.rule) - ruleSpecificity(a.rule) || a.index - b.index);\n  // return the {rule, index} pairs — don't collapse back to bare rules\n}\n```\n\n```tsx\n{ordered.map(({ rule, index }, position) => (\n  <RuleRow key={rule.id} rule={rule} position={position + 1}\n    onChange={(patch) => update(index, patch)} ... />\n))}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Caching opportunities\n\nEvery read in `lib/server/*.ts` calls `await connection()`, which is Next.js's explicit opt-out of prerendering/Data Cache — correct for a live console reading Postgres, but it means there is **zero caching layer** on any read, including screens that change rarely (`sources`, `rules`). Two concrete opportunities:\n\n1. **Request-level memoization.** These are raw Drizzle calls, not `fetch()`, so Next's automatic per-request fetch dedup doesn't apply. If a future change ever calls `getRunDetail(runId)` from two places in the same render tree (a layout + a page, or a page + a nested component), that's two round trips for identical data. Wrap the hot lookups in React's `cache()` now, while it's cheap, rather than after it becomes a duplicate-query bug:\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/runs.ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\n\nexport const getRunDetail = cache(async (runId: string): Promise<RunDetail | null> => {\n  // ...unchanged body\n});\n```\n\n`cache()` scopes to a single request/render pass, so it doesn't fight the \"always dynamic\" `connection()` design — it only removes duplicate work *within* one request.\n\n2. **Short revalidation for low-churn screens.** `sources` and `rules` change on human timescales (someone adds a source, someone saves a policy), not per-second like run status. `listSources`/`listRulesets` could use `unstable_cache` with a short `revalidateTag`, invalidated explicitly from `createSource`/`createRulesetVersion`, instead of paying a live Postgres round trip on every nav-rail click into those screens. Low priority — these are small tables — but free once the write paths already exist.\n\n3. **Engine-side (forward-looking):** AGENTS.md mandates prompt caching on the ruleset block as \"the single largest cost lever\" for the resolution agent. `engine/agent/prompts.py` is currently a stub — make sure the Anthropic `cache_control` breakpoint on the ruleset block lands in the first implementation, not as a follow-up; retrofitting cache breakpoints after prompt structure is fixed is more disruptive than designing for it from the start.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Memory leaks — none found\n\nChecked every `useEffect`/`setInterval`/event listener in the client components: `RunLive`'s elapsed-time interval clears on unmount and on `running` flip (`run-live.tsx:106`), the conflict queue's `keydown` listener is removed on unmount (`conflict-queue.tsx:131`), `ListRow`'s `scrollIntoView` effect has no dangling subscription. The DB client (`lib/db/index.ts`) correctly caches the pooled connection on `globalThis` to survive Next dev's HMR reloads rather than opening a new pool per reload — that's the right pattern, not a leak.\n\n**Forward-looking, for when `engine/db.py` is implemented:** FastAPI + `psycopg` under uvicorn needs an explicit connection pool (`psycopg_pool.AsyncConnectionPool`) opened once at app startup and reused — a per-request `psycopg.connect()` in a hot path is the classic way this leaks connections under load. Similarly, the FAISS index in `pipeline/blocking.py` should be built once per run and released, not rebuilt per query pair.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Redundant computations\n\nMinor but real, in `components/conflicts/conflict-sides.tsx:94`:\n\n```tsx\n{provenance(side) ? (\n  <p className=\"text-body text-g-500\">{provenance(side)}</p>   // called twice\n) : null}\n```\n\n`provenance()` runs twice per side, twice per row (4× per conflict row). Cheap (string concat), but free to fix and it's called on every row in a 100-row page:\n\n```tsx\nconst note = provenance(side);\nreturn note ? <p className=\"text-body text-g-500\">{note}</p> : null;\n```\n\nEverything else I checked in the per-row hot path — `diffSegments` (`lib/diff-chars.ts`, O(n) prefix/suffix scan on short field values), `SeverityHistogram`'s reduce/max over 4 fixed levels — is cheap enough that memoizing it would cost more than it saves.\n\n---\n\n**Priority order:** #2 (row memoization) is the one with real user-visible impact — it's the difference between the keyboard-driven review flow feeling instant at 400 conflicts vs. janky. The rest are small, mechanical fixes or forward-looking notes for the engine implementation.\n",
